        <p class="lead text-xl text-gray-700 not-prose mb-8">Sri Lanka packs an extraordinary density of wildlife into a small island. Despite being roughly the size of Ireland, it hosts leopards, elephants, sloth bears, blue whales, sperm whales, five species of marine turtle and over 400 bird species — including 33 species found nowhere else on earth. The national park system protects roughly 14% of the island's land area, and the best parks are genuinely world-class safari destinations that rank alongside far more famous East African reserves for specific experiences. This guide covers the six parks worth a dedicated visit, ranked by experience quality.</p>

        <h2>1. Yala National Park — Best for Leopard Sightings</h2>
        <p>Yala is the most visited national park in Sri Lanka, and for one dominant reason: it has the highest density of leopards of any national park in the world. Block 1, the most accessible section of the park, sees leopard sightings on most morning game drives — on a typical 3-hour safari you have a realistic 70–80% chance of seeing a leopard, which is simply extraordinary by any global standard. Beyond leopards, Yala holds elephants (120–150 individuals in Block 1), sloth bears, water buffaloes, crocodiles, jackals and over 200 bird species. The landscape is a dramatic mix of scrub jungle, rocky outcrops, lagoons and a long coastline that makes for beautiful photography at dawn and dusk. The main entrance at Palatupana is 280km from Colombo (5 hours) or 95km east of Tangalle (2 hours) — positioning Yala naturally at the end of a south coast road trip. Game drives run morning (5:30am–10am) and evening (3pm–6:30pm); morning gives better cat activity. Entry and jeep hire runs USD 25–35 per person for the park fees plus LKR 5,000–7,000 for a jeep. Book jeeps and drivers in advance between November and April.</p>

        <p><strong>Best time:</strong> February–July (dry season, animals concentrate near water). The park closes in September for maintenance. <strong>Stay at:</strong> Tissamaharama town for budget options; there are excellent luxury lodges just outside the park boundary.</p>

